findings.description:
bundler_audit.py's `_BLOCK_RE` (lines 7-17) requires `CVE:\s*(?P<cve>[^
]+)
` unconditionally, while the adjacent `GHSA:` field is explicitly optional (`(?:GHSA:\s*(?P<ghsa>[^
]+)
)?`, line 11) — the pattern's author evidently anticipated that GHSA-only advisory blocks (a real bundler-audit/ruby-advisory-db case for advisories that have not yet been assigned a CVE) can occur, but did not make the CVE field optional to match. BUNDLE_AUDIT_SAMPLE in tests/tools/test_bundler_audit.py contains only two blocks, both with a populated `CVE:` line, so this asymmetry is never exercised: there is no fixture for a block with `GHSA:` present and `CVE:` absent (or blank).

provenance.confirmation_reasoning:
Every mechanical assertion in the claim checks out. In /Volumes/Mini Vault/untitled_folder/projects/panopticon/skill/scripts/tools/bundler_audit.py the block pattern (lines 7-17) makes the GHSA line optional but the CVE line mandatory: line 10 is `r"CVE:\s*(?P<cve>[^
]+)
"` while line 11 is `r"(?:GHSA:\s*(?P<ghsa>[^
]+)
)?"`. The only unit fixture, BUNDLE_AUDIT_SAMPLE in /Volumes/Mini Vault/untitled_folder/projects/panopticon/tests/tools/test_bundler_audit.py (lines 9-27), has exactly two blocks, each with a populated `CVE:` line and a `GHSA:` line, so the GHSA-only shape is never fed to the parser. I grepped the whole tests tree for `bundler|Gemfile.lock|GHSA|Criticality`: the only other consumer is tests/tools/test_ruby_integration.py::test_bundler_audit_finds_railsgoat_vulns, which is triple skip-gated (fixture vendored, tool installed, rc in (0,1)) and asserts only `assertTrue(findings)` — it cannot catch a silently dropped block. Behaviourally the impact holds: `_BLOCK_RE.finditer()` anchors the literal `CVE:` immediately after the Version line, and no backtracking path lets `Version:\s*(?P<version>[^
]+)
` skip an intervening `GHSA:` line, so a CVE-less block matches nothing and the whole advisory (package, version, remediation) is dropped with no log or error — parse() has no else/unmatched-text handling (lines 31-64). Note `re.VERBOSE` on line 16 is inert here (the pattern contains no literal whitespace or `#`), so it neither helps nor hurts. What I could NOT verify from this repository is the premise that real bundler-audit ever emits a block with GHSA but no CVE; the repo's own design doc (docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-01-scanner-ecosystem-expansion.md lines 213-279) shows only CVE+GHSA samples and is where both the regex and the fixture were copied from verbatim. That external question governs how much this matters, hence LIKELY rather than CERTAIN. Two caveats on the claim's metadata: OWASP A03:2021-Injection is not applicable to a parser coverage gap, and the suggested remediation of making the CVE group optional needs care — with `CVE:` present but blank, greedy `\s*` already swallows the newline and captures the following `GHSA:` line as the cve value, producing a mangled rule_id rather than a clean omission.

